 - Response schema: `ChatRequest::with_response_schema(SchemaConstraint)` sends OpenAI-compatible `response_format` (`json_schema`) or, on Anthropic, a synthetic forced tool. Read the answer with `ChatResponse::schema_output()`. A schema cannot be combined with `tools` or `force_tool` (`LlmError::InvalidRequest`). Enforcement is the provider's; the crate does not re-validate the answer against the schema.
 - `breaker`: `CircuitBreaker` keyed by a string. Counts request outcomes (`Outcome::from_error`: transport, 5xx and 429 count; other errors are ignored), trips at 50% of the last 20 outcomes once there are 5, stays open 60 s, then allows one half-open probe. Independent of the breaker inside `FailoverChain`.
 - `health`: route health `Healthy` / `Degraded` (2 consecutive failures) / `Failed` (5), with a 60 s recovery window to `Degraded`. `RouteHealth` is the state machine; `record_success`, `record_failure`, `state` and `snapshot` use a process-wide registry keyed by route name. It retains at most 256 routes & 32 KiB of route-name bytes; recording an outcome or reading state refreshes recency. New routes evict least recently used observations to fit both limits. Evicted routes read as `Healthy` until reporting again; names larger than 32 KiB are ignored. `snapshot` lists only retained routes, sorted by name, without refreshing recency. Keep your own `RouteHealth` for lossless lifetime control; this registry does not change `FailoverChain` breakers.
